// ONE OPERATOR-DISPATCHED RUN OF ONE ROSTERED INSTRUMENT, ON THE FLEET, AND NOTHING ELSE.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS (operator ruling 2026-10-02). `gunbc test <label>` is the door to every instrument, but
// some subjects emit and build the whole compiler closure and then read the whole corpus, which no
// session container or free remote runner can hold within its limits -- the D13 dependency-demand
// census is the case that forced this, and joins the label list with its instrument row (gunbc#12992). The witnesses lanes carry fixed instruments on every pull request; this workflow carries
// none on any event. It is dispatched by hand at the head under test (`gh workflow run
// instrument-dispatch.yml --ref <branch> -f label=<label>`), so a change that needs an instrument's
// verdict at its own head -- D13's row-deletion change, which must execute the census at the tree it
// merges -- gets that run, and the run's artifact is what it cites.
//
// THE OPERATOR'S CONDITIONS, EACH HELD BY CONSTRUCTION HERE:
//   - workflow_dispatch is the ONLY trigger: `on` below has one arm, and nothing schedules, pushes or
//     reviews into it, so it costs the fleet nothing until someone asks.
//   - the input is a CLOSED choice over rostered labels: instrument_dispatch_labels names labels from
//     gunbc.instrument_targets, never a free string, and the input reaches the script only through the
//     step environment, never by interpolation into the script text.
//   - an explicit timeout, and a concurrency group so dispatches queue rather than stack on the
//     shared pool. QUEUE, AND NOT DROP: see instrument_dispatch_concurrency.
//   - contents: read and nothing else; no token is written to and none is persisted by the checkout.
//   - the run's revision, run id and the instrument's own output and exit are uploaded as an artifact,
//     seeded under RUNNER_TEMP before the fallible prelude so every termination uploads one.
//
// THE PRELUDE IS gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow's HOSTED BUILD PRELUDE, NOT A COPY OF IT: checkout,
// isolated toolchain homes, the pinned toolchain and the `gunbc` build are the steps emit-build already
// runs to reach `gunbc test`, so the two cannot disagree about how the door is built.

data instrument_dispatch_workflow_name: String = "instrument-dispatch"

// ONE RUN AT A TIME ON THE SHARED POOL, AND NO DISPATCH DROPPED. The group used to be the bare
// scalar form. Under it GitHub keeps ONE pending run per group and cancels the one already waiting
// when another arrives, with no signal to the lane that dispatched it: on 2026-10-03 a queued
// behavioral-equivalence run (37114501769) was cancelled before it started because another lane
// dispatched a different instrument ninety minutes later, and its owner learned of it only by
// reading the run list. A dispatched run is a receipt someone is waiting on, so losing one silently
// is a fail-open. The queue-max form keeps every pending run and still serializes them, which is
// the operator's condition held without the loss. One group for every label, not one per label:
// per-label groups would let two instruments run on the pool at once, which is the stacking the
// condition forbids.
//
// THE GROUP IS NOT THE WORKFLOW'S NAME, AND THAT IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE REPAIR. Concurrency is read
// from the workflow at the DISPATCHING ref, and one scalar-form dispatch cancels every pending run in
// its group whatever form those runs were dispatched with: the same day, three queue-max runs
// (37121808798, 37121810538, 37121812254) waited together for 88 minutes and were then all cancelled
// within three seconds by a dispatch from a ref still carrying `concurrency: instrument-dispatch`
// (37126626593). So the queue-max form protects a queue only from dispatches that also carry it, and
// every branch cut before this change still names the old group. Naming a group no pre-fix ref names
// puts those dispatches out of reach of this queue.
//
// WHAT THAT COSTS, BOUNDED: a pre-fix ref's dispatch runs in the OLD group, so while such refs still
// dispatch, one old-form run may execute alongside one run from this queue -- a relaxation of the
// queue-do-not-stack condition by at most one extra concurrent run. It dissolves when no pre-fix ref
// dispatches, which follows from the default branch carrying this form.
//
// THE SAME REMEDY, APPLIED AGAIN ON 2026-10-20 (M1 ruling): the group name 'instrument-dispatch-queued'
// is itself wedged in GitHub's state, repo-wide. Its head (39358338305, type-declaration-use-census)
// was CANCELLED by the fleet owner and the group did NOT unwedge -- runs created into it afterwards
// stayed pending with 0 jobs (39379535788 at main head 5f1bdd77a77 pending 6 min with 0 jobs;
// 39380660663 pending from 06:29Z, never promoted, cancelled; both measured 2026-10-20). Meanwhile
// FRESH key names promote correctly on the very same workflow: two back-to-back dispatches on the
// wave key instrument-dispatch-wave-base ran head-then-queued (39380951246 in_progress with a job at
// 06:32, 39380958633 pending 0 jobs), and the queued sibling PROMOTED to in_progress within a minute
// when the head completed at 07:00:08. A group name that GitHub holds wedged cannot be revived by
// cancelling its members -- so the serialized queue moves to a name NO earlier ref uses:
// 'instrument-dispatch-pool-serial' (checked: no commit of .github/workflows/instrument-dispatch.yml
// in this repository's history ever emitted that string). The old name appears nowhere in the emitted
// yml; the witness asserts the new one and the absence of the old.
data instrument_dispatch_concurrency_group: String = "instrument-dispatch-pool-serial"

// OPERATOR RULING 2026-10-20, SUPERSEDING THE ONE-AT-A-TIME CONDITION ABOVE. What it replaces, quoted
// from the paragraph it supersedes: "One group for every label, not one per label: per-label groups
// would let two instruments run on the pool at once, which is the stacking the condition forbids."
// The ruling admits CONCURRENCY under a declared cap: instrument dispatches may run at once up to a
// FIXED, BOUNDED set of concurrency-group keys, and max concurrency IS the number of keys. GitHub has
// no N-slot form, so the cap is spelled as a closed set of key LITERALS chosen by a comparison over
// the closed wave_shard choice -- never by interpolating a run id, a run number, or any free-text
// input into the key (a key that varies per run is no cap at all; a key interpolated from an input is
// bounded only by what a dispatcher can type). Every non-wave label keeps the one serialized group
// (instrument_dispatch_concurrency_group, fresh-named 2026-10-20) and stays serialized among itself
// and every other non-wave run; the
// wave gate's wave_shard choice maps to one key per CHOICE -- base, 0/4..K-1/K, merge/K -- derived
// from the same data row as K, so the wave contributes K + 2 keys and the total cap is K + 3.
// THE CAP, DECLARED: 7 (K = 4 from instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count, plus the one serialized
// queue). The roster: srv3 and srv4 each have 19 runners with ~11 busy, ~16 idle arm64 overall, so 7
// concurrent instrument runs leave v2-bootstrap's lanes unstarved (operator's reason, 2026-10-20).
// queue: max stays -- no dispatch is ever dropped; that half of the old condition is NOT superseded.
// EXTERNAL FACT (GitHub product policy, DESIGN 3): the EVALUATED concurrency group value is capped at
// 100 characters; every key this row can emit is far under it. The comparison values come only from
// the closed choice; a workflow_dispatch input description over 160 characters invalidates the whole
// file (recorded beside the row that emits one).
data instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix: String = "instrument-dispatch-wave-"

// Group NAMES carry only alphanumerics, '-' and '_' (GitHub product policy, DESIGN 3 external fact):
// a shard choice 'i/K' therefore keys as '<prefix>i-of-K', 'base' as '<prefix>base', 'merge/K' as
// '<prefix>merge-of-K'. The key is derived from the CHOICE, never from the run.

// THE CLOSED KEY SET, GENERATED FROM THE SAME ROW AS K: one key per wave_shard choice except 'none'
// (a wave run dispatched with 'none' refuses at the reader with 2 before its group matters; it falls
// into the serialized queue). Every key is a literal built here; nothing about a run flows into it.
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_concurrency_group_keys() -> List<String> {
  [join([instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix, "base"], "")]
    + list_map(xs: indices_below(n: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), f: fn(i) {
        join([instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix, to_string(i), "-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "")
      })
    + [join([instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix, "merge-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "")]
}

data instrument_dispatch_concurrency_cap: Int = length(instrument_dispatch_wave_concurrency_group_keys()) + 1

// THE BOUNDEDNESS PREDICATE, ASSERTED BY THE WITNESS OVER THE EMITTED KEYS: a closed key is a literal
// -- no expression braces, no context access, no input interpolation, not empty, and inside the group
// charset. A key that fails this is unbounded by construction (per-run-unique, free-text, or
// unrepresentable) and must not reach the yml. The witness REDs feed it the shapes the ruling forbids.
fn instrument_dispatch_concurrency_key_is_closed(key: String) -> Bool {
  length(key) > 0
    && !string_contains(s: key, pattern: "${{")
    && !string_contains(s: key, pattern: "inputs.")
    && !string_contains(s: key, pattern: "github.")
    && !string_contains(s: key, pattern: "/")
    && !string_contains(s: key, pattern: " ")
}

// THE GROUP EXPRESSION: a chain of ternaries choosing among the closed literals, defaulting to the
// serialized queue. The only dynamic values read are the CLOSED label and wave_shard choices, each
// compared against a generated literal -- never interpolated into a key. The label guard keeps a
// non-wave run dispatched with a wave_shard value out of the wave keys. '&&' binds tighter than the
// ternary in the GitHub expression grammar, so 'A && B ? k : rest' groups as '(A && B) ? k : rest'.
// THE GROUP EXPRESSION: a chain of '(guard && choice-match && KEY_LITERAL)' terms joined by '||',
// ending in the serialized-queue literal. GitHub's expression language has NO ternary (a '?' ':'
// makes the whole file invalid: observed at this repo, the push run dies jobless; DESIGN 3 external
// fact). '||' returns the first truthy operand and every key literal is a non-empty string, so the
// chain selects the first matching key and otherwise falls through to the queue -- and an empty key
// could never be selected, which is why the closedness predicate above also refuses the empty key.
// The only dynamic values read are the CLOSED label and wave_shard choices, each compared against a
// generated literal; nothing about a run, and no free text, ever enters a key.
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_concurrency_group_expression() -> String {
  let term = fn(choice, key) {
    join(["(inputs.", instrument_dispatch_label_input, " == '", render_label(l: instrument_dispatch_wave_label()), "' && inputs.", instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input, " == '", choice, "' && '", key, "')"], "")
  }
  let shard_terms = list_map(xs: indices_below(n: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), f: fn(i) {
    term(join([to_string(i), "/", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""), join([instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix, to_string(i), "-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""))
  })
  let all_terms = [term("base", join([instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix, "base"], ""))]
    + shard_terms
    + [term(join(["merge/", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""), join([instrument_dispatch_concurrency_wave_group_prefix, "merge-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "")),
       join(["'", instrument_dispatch_concurrency_group, "'"], "")]
  join(["${{ ", fold(all_terms, init: "", f: fn(acc, t) {
    if length(acc) == 0 { t } else { join([acc, " || ", t], "") }
  }), " }}"], "")
}

data instrument_dispatch_concurrency: ConcurrencySpec = ConcurrencyMappingQueueMax {
  group: instrument_dispatch_wave_concurrency_group_expression(),
  cancel_in_progress: Present { value: QueueMaxCancelInProgressFalse }
}

data instrument_dispatch_job_id: String = "instrument"

data instrument_dispatch_label_input: String = "label"

data instrument_dispatch_directory: String = "instrument-dispatch"

data instrument_dispatch_artifact_name: String = "instrument-dispatch-receipt"

// Role-tagged artifact names. EXTERNAL FACT (observed 2026-10-20, runs 39387420882 + 39398874591):
// for this repo/app token the workflow-runs API returns inputs=NULL on BOTH the list and the per-run
// endpoints, so a run cannot be told apart by its dispatch inputs from inside a job -- discovery
// keyed on inputs sees zero candidates and the chain silently starves (this cost four shard runs
// their base). The artifact-list endpoint DOES carry reliable name, workflow_run.id,
// workflow_run.head_sha and created_at (status/conclusion arrive null there), and its name= filter
// is EXACT (a substring query returns nothing). So each wave role uploads under its own suffixed
// name, generated from the same K row as everything else, and cross-run discovery is an exact-name
// query -- closed, never free text. Status is deliberately NOT a discovery filter: every receipt
// header carries its own status field and the READER refuses a non-zero one, so the transport never
// becomes the authority on validity -- it hands over candidates; content checks in the lookup are
// a second guard, the reader remains the first.
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_artifact_roles() -> List<String> {
  ["base"]
    + list_map(xs: indices_below(n: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), f: fn(i) {
        join([to_string(i), "-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "")
      })
    + [join(["merge-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "")]
}

// (wave_shard choice, artifact role) generated together from the K row; the two parallel folds
// below build choices and roles with the SAME index, so they cannot drift apart.
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_artifact_name(role: String) -> String {
  join([instrument_dispatch_artifact_name, "-", role], "")
}

// The upload name as a closed ${{-chain over the same choice literals as the wave_shard input:
// each wave role uploads its own artifact; every other label keeps the plain name untouched.
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_upload_name_expression() -> String {
  let term = fn(choice, role) {
    join(["(inputs.", instrument_dispatch_label_input, " == '", render_label(l: instrument_dispatch_wave_label()), "' && inputs.", instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input, " == '", choice, "' && '", instrument_dispatch_wave_artifact_name(role), "')"], "")
  }
  let base_term = term("base", "base")
  let shard_terms = list_map(xs: indices_below(n: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), f: fn(i) {
    term(join([to_string(i), "/", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""), join([to_string(i), "-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""))
  })
  let merge_term = term(join(["merge/", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""), join(["merge-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], ""))
  let default_term = join(["'", instrument_dispatch_artifact_name, "'"], "")
  let all_terms = [base_term] + shard_terms + [merge_term, default_term]
  join(["${{ ", fold(all_terms, init: "", f: fn(acc, t) {
    if length(acc) == 0 { t } else { join([acc, " || ", t], "") }
  }), " }}"], "")
}


data instrument_dispatch_retention_days: Int = 30

// A bound, not a budget: the census emits and builds the compiler closure (about 35 minutes measured on
// emit-build) and then reads the whole corpus; a timeout that cut an honest run would turn its verdict
// into silence.
data instrument_dispatch_timeout_minutes: Int = 240

// THE WAVE DISPATCH, AS A CLOSED CHOICE (T0.2, the transport half of the namespace-wave admission
// vertical; the identity model is gunbc.instruments.namespace_wave_binding_diff's, sunny-bee-153's
// authority). A wave run takes one of three SHAPES named by the reader's GUNBC_WAVE_SHARD: `base`
// (the once-per-main-revision base census, reused by every later wave run), `i/K` (one shard of the
// head census), `merge/K` (the join that alone mints the verdict -- the only admission token). The
// input `wave_shard` is a closed InputChoice, never free-text shell, and its options are GENERATED
// from one data row below, so the choice and the K cannot drift without the gate noticing. K stays
// the READER's authority at judgment time (a merge whose receipt population disagrees with the K in
// the shard headers refuses with 2); this row bounds only what can be asked for.
// `none` is the default and names NOT A WAVE RUN: a non-wave label is unaffected by the input, and a
// wave label dispatched without naming a shard reaches the reader as the empty mode, which its
// parse_shard_mode refuses as malformed with exit 2 -- a forgotten input fails closed, it never
// silently becomes a whole-wave run.
data instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count: Int = 4

data instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input: String = "wave_shard"

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_label() -> Label {
  namespace_wave_binding_diff_label()
}

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_options() -> List<String> {
  ["none", "base"] + list_map(xs: indices_below(n: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), f: fn(i) { join([to_string(i), "/", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "") }) + [join(["merge/", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count)], "")]
}

// THE INPUT'S AUTHORITY, SMALLER THAN THE GATE'S OWN: the choice only names a shape. The reader owns
// whether that shape is coherent -- head, base id, wave, rewritten set, K, every check refuses (2).
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_is_wave_expression() -> String {
  join(["inputs.", instrument_dispatch_label_input, " == '", render_label(l: instrument_dispatch_wave_label()), "' && inputs.", instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input, " != 'none'"], "")
}

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_expression() -> String {
  join(["${{ inputs.", instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input, " }}"], "")
}

// THE DISPATCHABLE LABELS: instruments whose subject is too large for any per-change lane and is run
// on purpose. The round-trip battery is the first (its own row says it is run by name, not a merge
// gate). Each is a row of gunbc.instrument_targets (instrument_dispatch_labels_are_rostered), and
// adding one is a row here. The self-host behavioral-equivalence receipt series is the second: one
// emitted crate built per roster module exceeds the free remote runner's one-hour cap, and its
// receipt is taken on purpose (gunbc.self_host_behavioral_equivalence_receipt), never per change.
// The type-declaration-use census (the nominal-type plan's M0) is another: it emits and builds the
// compiler, then resolves and infers the whole corpus, past any per-change lane and the same hour cap.
// The native refusal census is another: `census-infer` emits and builds the compiler closure and then
// resolves and infers every indexed module, which outlives the free remote runner's one-hour cap twice
// over, and its saved whole-tree receipt is what the frontier root ranking and the census lane's step D
// read. The stage0 regen round's price is another: a seed build plus a whole-population emit and a
// one-mirror emit, which needs a memory-bounded slot the free remote runner does not expose (its
// host budget is unreadable there and the run refuses). A dispatchable label is on-demand only; it
// adds no job to any per-change workflow.
fn instrument_dispatch_labels() -> List<Label> {
  [
    dag_emit_real_grammar_round_trips_label(), self_host_behavioral_equivalence_label(), dependency_demand_census_label(),
    v2_native_census_label(), type_declaration_use_census_label(), regen_round_cost_label(),
    compute_run_unit_cold_label(), compute_run_unit_sccache_probe_label(), compute_run_unit_cold_same_path_label(), compute_wrong_capture_label(),
    compute_dependency_wrong_capture_label(),
    instrument_dispatch_wave_label(),
  ]
}

fn instrument_dispatch_label_options() -> List<String> {
  list_map(xs: instrument_dispatch_labels(), f: fn(l) { render_label(l: l) })
}

fn instrument_dispatch_labels_are_rostered() -> Bool {
  let rostered = list_map(xs: instrument_targets(), f: fn(l) { render_label(l: l) })
  length(xs: filter(xs: instrument_dispatch_label_options(), predicate: fn(o) {
    length(xs: filter(xs: rostered, predicate: fn(r) { r == o })) == 0
  })) == 0
}

// THE MEMORY A DISPATCHED SUBJECT NEEDS IS A DECLARED FACT OF ITS LABEL, NOT A FACT OF WHICHEVER HOST
// PICKS THE JOB UP (follow-up to gunbc#13141). A whole-tree census held run 37119024146 on srv1 because
// that slot's cgroup happened to admit it; nothing said it needed to. Each row here names a label and
// the memory its run must be granted, and the requirement is held twice from this one row:
//   - at GENERATION, against the selected runner target's memory regime
//     (gunbc.ci.ci_runner_target ci_runner_target_ram_speed_budget -- the existing vocabulary, so a
//     runner switch that cannot hold a row refuses the workflow instead of emitting it);
//   - at RUN TIME, by a guard step bound to that label which reads the job's effective memory bound
//     (the least numeric memory.high/memory.max on its cgroup path, and MemTotal) and refuses before
//     the prelude spends anything, so an under-provisioned runner stops loudly rather than OOM-killing
//     the instrument at hour two.
// The demand is per label, not one job-wide number: a gate sized for the largest subject refuses every
// smaller one (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_gate_sized_for_the_largest_subject_refuses_every_smaller_one).
// A label with no row carries no memory wall; the rows cover the whole-tree instruments, which are the
// subjects whose demand scales with the corpus.
type InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand {
  label: Label
  demand: ByteSize
}

// 20 GiB: an explicit policy budget for a whole-tree pass (about 20GB, gunbc#13141 and the follow-up
// that raised this row), sized under the fleet slot's memory.high so the selected target admits it.
data instrument_dispatch_whole_tree_memory_demand: ByteSize = byte_size(21474836480)

// 16 GiB: the stage0 regen round's policy budget. Its subject is the seed closure's whole-population
// emit, whose measured peak sits near 12.9 GiB on the fleet (gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow
// compiler_gate_stage0_regen_step, the memory note), plus headroom for the seed build's children.
data instrument_dispatch_stage0_regen_memory_demand: ByteSize = byte_size(17179869184)

// Derived from gunbc.instruments.compute_run_unit_cold compute_run_unit_cold_job_memory_demand:
// unit grant plus declared launcher headroom. Not a second 16 GiB literal, and not the regen row
// (DESIGN §3 meaning fork).
data instrument_dispatch_compute_run_unit_cold_memory_demand: ByteSize = compute_run_unit_cold_job_memory_demand()

// 16 GiB: the wave gate's policy budget, from the MEASURED work, not a borrowed number. The base
// step's peak RSS measured ~14 GB (about 13.04 GiB) on the fleet while its head-census companion
// held 11.7 GB; 16 GiB is that peak plus about 21 percent headroom for the census' emit-and-build
// children, and it sits under the fleet slot's memory.high (21 GiB, gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired
// the 2026-08-27 ruling), so the selected target admits the row and the run-time guard still refuses
// loudly on an under-provisioned runner before the prelude spends anything. The 240 minutes below is
// a DECLARED workload budget for this subject, not an adaptation: the base step alone measured 28
// minutes, every shard is bounded by 240, and a refusal is never answered by widening it.
data instrument_dispatch_wave_memory_demand: ByteSize = byte_size(17179869184)

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_demands() -> List<InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand> {
  [
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: regen_round_cost_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_stage0_regen_memory_demand },
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: compute_run_unit_cold_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_compute_run_unit_cold_memory_demand },
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: compute_run_unit_cold_same_path_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_compute_run_unit_cold_memory_demand },
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: compute_wrong_capture_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_compute_run_unit_cold_memory_demand },
    // The dependency arm is the same subject at the same price: two clean release builds of the
    // same work_default_build() operation and two captures. It shares the compute row, not the
    // whole-tree row (DESIGN §3 meaning fork).
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: compute_dependency_wrong_capture_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_compute_run_unit_cold_memory_demand },
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: instrument_dispatch_wave_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_wave_memory_demand },
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: dependency_demand_census_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_whole_tree_memory_demand },
    InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand { label: v2_native_census_label(), demand: instrument_dispatch_whole_tree_memory_demand },
  ]
}

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_demands_fit(demands: List<InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand>, budget: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  length(xs: filter(xs: demands, predicate: fn(d) { byte_size_count(b: d.demand) > byte_size_count(b: budget) })) == 0
}

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_demands_fit_selected_target() -> Bool {
  instrument_dispatch_memory_demands_fit(demands: instrument_dispatch_memory_demands(), budget: ci_runner_target_ram_speed_budget(target: selected_ci_runner_target()))
}

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_demands_are_dispatchable() -> Bool {
  let options = instrument_dispatch_label_options()
  length(xs: filter(xs: instrument_dispatch_memory_demands(), predicate: fn(d) {
    length(xs: filter(xs: options, predicate: fn(o) { o == render_label(l: d.label) })) == 0
  })) == 0
}

// THE GUARD IS ONE SCRIPT, EMITTED THROUGH v2.workflow.bash_emit LIKE ITS SIBLINGS; each declared row
// binds it to a label and hands it the row's byte count through the step environment, so the number has
// one home (the row) and the script one emission. It refuses when the machine's memory (free -b) or a
// numeric memory.high / memory.max on the job's own cgroup is below the requirement. A bound set only on
// an ANCESTOR cgroup of the step is not read: bash_emit carries no loop to walk the path, and the
// runner's bound sits on the runner unit's own cgroup, which its steps inherit.
fn instrument_dispatch_memory_refusal(source: String) -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_with_redir(
      inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "echo"),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "::error::InstrumentMemoryBudgetUnmet label="),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "INSTRUMENT_LABEL"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: join([" ", source, "="], "")),
          bash_build_word_var(name: source),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: " required="),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "REQUIRED_MEMORY_BYTES"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: " -- this runner cannot hold the dispatched subject (gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow instrument_dispatch_memory_demands)"),
        ]),
      ]),
      redir: ^bash_production_fold_redir_stdout_to_stderr,
    ),
    bash_build_exit(code: "1"),
  ]
}

fn instrument_dispatch_cgroup_read(file: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: bash_build_or_else(
    left: bash_build_with_redir(
      inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "cat"),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "/sys/fs/cgroup"),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "cg"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: join(["/", file], "")),
        ]),
      ]),
      redir: ^bash_production_fold_redir_stderr_null,
    ),
    right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["echo", "max"]),
  ))
}

fn instrument_dispatch_below_required(var: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_test_bracket(words: [bash_build_word_var(name: var), bash_build_word_lit(text: "-lt"), bash_build_word_var(name: "REQUIRED_MEMORY_BYTES")])
}

fn instrument_dispatch_cgroup_bound_below_required(var: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_and_then(
    left: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [bash_build_word_var(name: var), bash_build_word_lit(text: "!="), bash_build_word_lit(text: "max")]),
    right: instrument_dispatch_below_required(var: var),
  )
}

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_guard_statements() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_assign(name: "machine", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: bash_build_pipe(
      left: bash_build_pipe(
        left: bash_build_pipe(left: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["free", "-b"]), right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["grep", "^Mem:"])),
        right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["tr", "-s", " "]),
      ),
      right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["cut", "-d", " ", "-f", "2"]),
    ))),
    bash_build_assign(name: "cg", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: bash_build_pipe(
      left: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["cut", "-d", ":", "-f", "3", "/proc/self/cgroup"]),
      right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["head", "-n", "1"]),
    ))),
    bash_build_assign(name: "memory_high", value: instrument_dispatch_cgroup_read(file: "memory.high")),
    bash_build_assign(name: "memory_max", value: instrument_dispatch_cgroup_read(file: "memory.max")),
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "echo"),
      bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "instrument memory: machine="),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "machine"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: " cgroup="),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "cg"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: " memory.high="),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "memory_high"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: " memory.max="),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "memory_max"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: " required="),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "REQUIRED_MEMORY_BYTES"),
      ]),
    ]),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(cond: instrument_dispatch_below_required(var: "machine"), then_stmts: instrument_dispatch_memory_refusal(source: "machine")),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(cond: instrument_dispatch_cgroup_bound_below_required(var: "memory_high"), then_stmts: instrument_dispatch_memory_refusal(source: "memory_high")),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(cond: instrument_dispatch_cgroup_bound_below_required(var: "memory_max"), then_stmts: instrument_dispatch_memory_refusal(source: "memory_max")),
  ]
}

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_guard_script() -> Outcome<String> {
  bash_emit_stmts(statements: instrument_dispatch_memory_guard_statements(), set_e: true)
}

fn instrument_dispatch_memory_guard_step(d: InstrumentDispatchMemoryDemand, guard: String) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: join(["Require the declared memory for ", render_label(l: d.label)], "") },
    id: none,
    run: guard,
    shell: Present { value: Bash },
    env: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "INSTRUMENT_LABEL", value: yaml_string(s: instrument_dispatch_label_expression())),
        kv(key: "REQUIRED_MEMORY_BYTES", value: yaml_string(s: to_string(byte_size_count(b: d.demand)))),
      ]
    },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: join(["inputs.", instrument_dispatch_label_input, " == '", render_label(l: d.label), "'"], "") },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none,
  }
}

fn instrument_dispatch_label_expression() -> String {
  join(["${{ inputs.", instrument_dispatch_label_input, " }}"], "")
}

// THE RECEIPT LIVES UNDER RUNNER_TEMP, NOT THE CHECKOUT, so it exists before the checkout does: the
// seed step that writes it runs first, and nothing the prelude does -- a failed checkout, toolchain
// install or build -- can leave the always() upload with nothing to read (review of gunbc#12998).
fn instrument_dispatch_dir_word() -> Node {
  bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [bash_build_word_var(name: "RUNNER_TEMP"), bash_build_word_lit(text: join(["/", instrument_dispatch_directory], ""))])
}

fn instrument_dispatch_path_word(file: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [bash_build_word_var(name: "RUNNER_TEMP"), bash_build_word_lit(text: join(["/", instrument_dispatch_directory, "/", file], ""))])
}

fn instrument_dispatch_upload_path() -> String {
  join(["${{ runner.temp }}/", instrument_dispatch_directory], "")
}

fn instrument_dispatch_echo_to(words: List<Node>, file: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_with_redir_to_file(
    inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "echo"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: words)]),
    path: instrument_dispatch_path_word(file: file),
  )
}

// THE SEED: the receipt line, and placeholders the instrument step overwrites. A run that never reaches
// the instrument uploads stdout, stderr and exit `not-started`, never an absent artifact and never
// a fabricated exit code.
fn instrument_dispatch_seed_statements() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "mkdir"), bash_build_word_lit(text: "-p"), instrument_dispatch_dir_word()]),
    instrument_dispatch_echo_to(words: [
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "instrument-dispatch label="),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "INSTRUMENT_LABEL"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: " revision="),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "GITHUB_SHA"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: " run="),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "GITHUB_RUN_ID"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: " attempt="),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT"),
    ], file: "receipt.txt"),
    instrument_dispatch_echo_to(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "not-started")], file: "stdout.txt"),
    instrument_dispatch_echo_to(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "not-started")], file: "stderr.txt"),
    instrument_dispatch_echo_to(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "exit=not-started")], file: "exit.txt"),
  ]
}

fn instrument_dispatch_seed_script() -> Outcome<String> {
  bash_emit_stmts(statements: instrument_dispatch_seed_statements(), set_e: true)
}

data instrument_dispatch_seed_step_name: String = "Seed the run's receipt before anything that can fail"

fn instrument_dispatch_seed_step(script: String) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: instrument_dispatch_seed_step_name },
    id: Present { value: "seed_receipt" },
    run: script,
    shell: Present { value: Bash },
    env: Present { value: [kv(key: "INSTRUMENT_LABEL", value: yaml_string(s: instrument_dispatch_label_expression()))] },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none,
  }
}

// THE RUN SCRIPT. The seed step has already written the receipt; this overwrites the placeholders with
// the instrument's stdout and stderr and its exit, and the step fails exactly when the instrument did
// not hold. stderr reaches the log AS THE INSTRUMENT WRITES IT (tee into stderr.txt), so a multi-hour
// census shows its per-module progress live and a cancelled run's stderr.txt and stdout.txt hold what
// was written before the cancel for the always() upload; stdout is replayed after exit. pipefail makes
// `status` the instrument's exit rather than tee's. stderr is captured, not left on the log alone:
// `gunbc test` writes its verdict there on a non-zero exit, so a run that did not hold would otherwise
// upload an empty stdout and keep its receipt only in the lossy job log.
fn instrument_dispatch_statements() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_assign_lit(name: "status", text: "0"),
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "set"), bash_build_word_lit(text: "-o"), bash_build_word_lit(text: "pipefail")]),
    bash_build_or_else(
      left: bash_build_pipe(
        left: bash_build_with_redir(
          inner: bash_build_subshell_from_stmts(stmts: [
            bash_build_with_redir_to_file(
              inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
                bash_build_word_lit(text: "target/release/gunbc"),
                bash_build_word_lit(text: "test"),
                bash_build_word_var(name: "INSTRUMENT_LABEL"),
              ]),
              path: instrument_dispatch_path_word(file: "stdout.txt"),
            ),
          ]),
          redir: ^bash_production_fold_redir_stderr_to_stdout,
        ),
        right: bash_build_with_redir(
          inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "tee"), instrument_dispatch_path_word(file: "stderr.txt")]),
          redir: ^bash_production_fold_redir_stdout_to_stderr,
        ),
      ),
      right: bash_build_assign_var(name: "status", var_name: "?"),
    ),
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "cat"), instrument_dispatch_path_word(file: "stdout.txt")]),
    instrument_dispatch_echo_to(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "exit="), bash_build_word_var(name: "status")], file: "exit.txt"),
    bash_build_test_bracket(words: [bash_build_word_var(name: "status"), bash_build_word_lit(text: "="), bash_build_word_lit(text: "0")]),
  ]
}

fn instrument_dispatch_script() -> Outcome<String> {
  bash_emit_stmts(statements: instrument_dispatch_statements(), set_e: true)
}

fn instrument_dispatch_run_step(script: String) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Run the dispatched instrument" },
    id: Present { value: "instrument" },
    run: script,
    shell: Present { value: Bash },
    env: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "INSTRUMENT_LABEL", value: yaml_string(s: instrument_dispatch_label_expression())),
        // THE TYPED INPUT MAPPED TO THE READER'S POLICY NAME, never to free-text shell: the value can
        // only be one of the generated options (GitHub enforces the choice) and reaches the reader as
        // an environment variable, never interpolated into a command line. The reader parses the mode
        // itself. A wave label dispatched with the 'none' default reaches parse_shard_mode as 'none',
        // which it refuses as malformed with exit 2 -- a forgotten input fails closed and never
        // silently becomes a whole-wave run. Other labels' hosts do not read this name; for them the
        // variable is inert.
        kv(key: "GUNBC_WAVE_SHARD", value: yaml_string(s: join(["${{ inputs.", instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input, " }}"], ""))),
      ]
    },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none,
  }
}

// The receipt is uploaded on every termination: a did-not-hold run is exactly when it is wanted.
fn instrument_dispatch_upload_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Upload the run's receipt and output" },
    id: none,
    uses: upload_artifact_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "name", value: yaml_string(s: instrument_dispatch_wave_upload_name_expression())),
        kv(key: "path", value: yaml_string(s: instrument_dispatch_upload_path())),
        kv(key: "if-no-files-found", value: yaml_string(s: "error")),
        kv(key: "retention-days", value: yaml_int(n: instrument_dispatch_retention_days)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "always()" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none,
  }
}

// THE CROSS-RUN RECEIPT TRANSPORT (T0.2; the seam agreed with sunny-bee-153, selection rule per M1's
// ruling 2026-10-19). THE READER JUDGES, THE TRANSPORT MOVES: this step only puts files into the
// reader's receipt directory before the instrument starts. It never judges identity -- the reader's
// parse and merge refuse (2) on every mismatch (base id, instrument revision, census contract, head,
// wave, rewritten set, K, duplicates, strays). It never fills a gap either: a run that has no base
// yet gets an empty directory and the reader's own missing-base refusal names what is absent.
//
// WHICH BASE, BY NAME, NOT BY RECENCY (M1's ruling): a shard or merge run downloads the NEWEST
// completed `base` dispatch of THIS workflow at THIS head whose artifact carries a base-<main rev>-*
// file, where <main rev> is the merge base of HEAD and origin/main -- the same value the reader
// computes (census_merge_base in the host; ambient_census_base_ref names the ref). Recency alone
// would refuse a spurious 2 whenever the newest base belongs to another main revision while a
// correct one exists; name-selection refuses only when no correct base exists at all.
//
// HOW, WITHOUT gh ON THE SLOT: python3 + urllib (stdlib json) against the artifacts API, the fleet's
// idiom for API work on a slot (the fleet's release-bins lookup does the same HTTP calls with curl;
// python3 on this image does the JSON). The token is the run's own, scope-limited to this repo and
// -- per the operator ruling 2026-10-19 -- read-only for run artifacts.
// The program itself, assembled from constants and the two policy values (the workflow file it may
// read runs of, and the instrument label whose base it may consume) -- policy reaches the program by
// concatenation from this module, never from a run input.
fn instrument_dispatch_wave_lookup_program() -> String {
  join([
    // The program runs under bash (the step's shell) via a quoted heredoc into python3: no shell
    // expansion inside, and the GH default `bash -e` propagates any python failure to the step, so
    // a transport that cannot stage refuses BEFORE the instrument runs.
    "python3 - <<'PYEOF'\n",
    "import glob, json, os, shutil, subprocess, tempfile, urllib.request, urllib.error, zipfile\n",
    "HEAD = os.environ['GITHUB_SHA']\n",
    "REPO = os.environ['GITHUB_REPOSITORY']\n",
    "RUN = int(os.environ['GITHUB_RUN_ID'])\n",
    "TOKEN = os.environ['GITHUB_TOKEN']\n",
    "MODE = os.environ['GUNBC_WAVE_SHARD']\n",
    "STAGING = os.environ['WAVE_RECEIPT_STAGING']\n",
    "WORKFLOW_FILE = '", instrument_dispatch_workflow_name, ".yml'\n",
    "def api(path):\n",
    "    req = urllib.request.Request('https://api.github.com' + path, headers={'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + TOKEN, 'Accept': 'application/vnd.github+json'})\n",
    "    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=60) as r:\n",
    "        return json.load(r)\n",
    // EXTERNAL FACTS (GitHub product policy, DESIGN 3; observed on the fleet at head 388b9d0803, run
    // 39374224295): (1) `git fetch` over smart HTTP REJECTS the workflow token's Bearer scheme --
    // GitHub wants Basic there -- so a fetch with http.extraHeader=Authorization: Bearer dies at 128
    // ('could not read Username'); (2) the slot checkout is shallow (fetch-depth 1) and
    // persist-credentials:false, so the merge base of HEAD and main is neither present nor fetchable
    // by plain git. Both handled here: the merge base is read from GitHub's OWN graph (the compare
    // API's merge_base_commit.sha -- the same fact `git merge-base` computes; the reader checks
    // receipts against its OWN computation regardless, so a disagreement can only refuse, never
    // admit), and the objects the reader's base step needs are PRE-FETCHED with Basic auth, with the
    // same auth persisted for the reader's own plain fetches: HOME is RUNNER_TEMP for the whole job
    // (the isolation step), so a --global git config writes into the job's temp dir, cleaned when the
    // job ends, carrying only the run-scoped token, which expires with the run. A failure at any
    // point here refuses BEFORE the instrument runs (bash -e on the heredoc): fail-closed -- the
    // transport never lets the instrument guess at a missing base.
    // The base a wave is diffed against is the READER's policy fact, not the transport's: it is read
    // from namespace_wave_binding_diff.binding_diff_base_ref() (which delegates to the census base
    // ref). Normally 'origin/main'; a simulation head may set it to an arbitrary commit. GitHub's
    // compare API names branches/sha by their own spelling, so a leading 'origin/' is trimmed here --
    // a transport-side spelling of the reader's fact, never a second authority (the reader still
    // judges every receipt against its OWN computation; a disagreement can only refuse, never admit).
    // EXTERNAL FACT (fleet, chain at 538faf3a45c): the slot checkout is fetch-depth 1, so NEITHER end
    // of the merge base has parentage; the reader's own `git merge-base` needs ancestors on BOTH
    // sides. ensure_objects therefore fetches the base by exact sha WITH ancestry (depth 50) and
    // deepens the HEAD side once, always -- both cheap (~100 commits), turning 'unreached' refusals
    // into observations. Comments live outside the emitted list: bare lines inside it leak verbatim.
    "import base64 as _b64\n",
    "SERVER = os.environ['GITHUB_SERVER_URL']\n",
    "BASE_REF = '", binding_diff_base_ref(), "'.replace('origin/', '')\n",
    "def auth_header():\n",
    "    return 'Authorization: Basic ' + _b64.b64encode(('x-access-token:' + TOKEN).encode()).decode()\n",
    "def ensure_objects(rev):\n",
    "    subprocess.run(['git', 'config', '--global', '--replace-all', 'http.' + SERVER + '/.extraHeader', auth_header()], check=True)\n",
    "    subprocess.run(['git', '-c', 'http.extraHeader=' + auth_header(), 'fetch', '--quiet', '--depth=50', 'origin', rev], check=False)\n",
    "    subprocess.run(['git', '-c', 'http.extraHeader=' + auth_header(), 'fetch', '--quiet', '--deepen=500', 'origin'], check=False)\n",
    "    shallow = os.path.exists(os.path.join('.git', 'shallow'))\n",
    "    for _round in range(8):\n",
    "        probe = subprocess.run(['git', 'cat-file', '-t', rev], capture_output=True, text=True)\n",
    "        if probe.returncode == 0 and probe.stdout.strip() == 'commit':\n",
    "            return\n",
    "        subprocess.run(['git', '-c', 'http.extraHeader=' + auth_header(), 'fetch', '--quiet', '--depth=500', 'origin', BASE_REF + ':refs/remotes/origin/' + BASE_REF], check=True)\n",
    "        if shallow:\n",
    "            subprocess.run(['git', '-c', 'http.extraHeader=' + auth_header(), 'fetch', '--quiet', '--deepen=500', 'origin'], check=False)\n",
    "    raise SystemExit('wave-transport: the merge base object is unreachable after 8 deepen rounds -- refusing before the instrument runs')\n",
    "def merge_base():\n",
    "    return api('/repos/' + REPO + '/compare/' + BASE_REF + '...' + HEAD)['merge_base_commit']['sha']\n",
    // A head shard belongs to one head, so shard candidates are restricted to THIS head; a base is
    // a fact of (main revision, instrument revision) reused by every wave run (ruling 2026-10-20:
    // select the base artifact by the main revision in its file name, newest among those), so base
    // candidates are searched across heads.
    "def clear(dest):\n",
    "    os.makedirs(dest, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "    for old in os.listdir(dest):\n",
    "        p = os.path.join(dest, old)\n",
    "        shutil.rmtree(p) if os.path.isdir(p) else os.remove(p)\n",
    // Discovery EXTERNAL FACT (2026-10-20, runs 39387420882 / 39398874591): inputs are NULL for
    // every run on BOTH runs endpoints under this app token, so roles come only from the suffixed
    // artifact names (upload is the closed ${{-chain above; the name= filter is EXACT). Reliable
    // fields: name, workflow_run.id, workflow_run.head_sha, created_at -- status/conclusion arrive
    // NULL and are NOT filters: receipt headers carry their own status and the reader refuses a
    // non-zero one. The repo artifacts endpoint is tried first (one call); on any HTTP error the
    // fallback enumerates recent workflow runs and asks each run's own artifacts -- the endpoints
    // this fleet has actually served. fetch() requires the exact artifact name AND a must-match
    // file inside the zip before declaring success, so a wrong-revision candidate is skipped
    // rather than staged; the probe dir lives outside STAGING, so skipping never leaves a stray in
    // the reader's receipt dir and never touches already-staged shards. Probe attempts are capped:
    // past that many of the freshest base runs, staleness is likely; the reader then refuses
    // (unobserved, 2) -- fail-closed, no guessing.
    "ARTIFACT_PREFIX = '", instrument_dispatch_artifact_name, "'\n",
    "SHARD_ROLES = [", fold(list_map(xs: indices_below(n: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), f: fn(i) { join(["'", to_string(i), "-of-", to_string(instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_count), "'"], "") }), init: "", f: fn(acc, r) {
      if length(acc) == 0 { r } else { join([acc, ", ", r], "") }
    }), "]\n",
    "def role_artifact_name(role):\n",
    "    return ARTIFACT_PREFIX + '-' + role\n",
    "def artifact_candidates(role, head_ok):\n",
    "    target = role_artifact_name(role)\n",
    "    out = []\n",
    "    try:\n",
    "        for a in api('/repos/' + REPO + '/actions/artifacts?name=' + target + '&per_page=100')['artifacts']:\n",
    "            wr = a.get('workflow_run') or {}\n",
    "            rid = wr.get('id')\n",
    "            if rid is None or rid == RUN:\n",
    "                continue\n",
    "            if head_ok and (wr.get('head_sha') or '') != HEAD:\n",
    "                continue\n",
    "            out.append((str(a.get('created_at') or ''), rid))\n",
    "        out.sort(reverse=True)\n",
    "        return out\n",
    "    except urllib.error.HTTPError:\n",
    "        pass\n",
    "    for w in api('/repos/' + REPO + '/actions/workflows/' + WORKFLOW_FILE + '/runs?per_page=100')['workflow_runs']:\n",
    "        rid = w.get('id')\n",
    "        if rid is None or rid == RUN:\n",
    "            continue\n",
    "        if head_ok and w.get('head_sha') != HEAD:\n",
    "            continue\n",
    "        try:\n",
    "            arts = api('/repos/' + REPO + '/actions/runs/' + str(rid) + '/artifacts')['artifacts']\n",
    "        except urllib.error.HTTPError:\n",
    "            continue\n",
    "        if any(a.get('name') == target and not a.get('expired') for a in arts):\n",
    "            out.append((str(w.get('created_at') or ''), rid))\n",
    "    out.sort(reverse=True)\n",
    "    return out\n",
    "class _StripAuthRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):\n",
    "    def redirect_request(self, req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):\n",
    "        return urllib.request.Request(newurl, headers={}, method=req.get_method())\n",
    "def download(url, path):\n",
    "    opener = urllib.request.build_opener(_StripAuthRedirect)\n",
    "    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + TOKEN})\n",
    "    with opener.open(req, timeout=600) as r, open(path, 'wb') as f:\n",
    "        while True:\n",
    "            chunk = r.read(1048576)\n",
    "            if not chunk:\n",
    "                break\n",
    "            f.write(chunk)\n",
    // SINGLE LOCATION, TYPED DUPLICATE REFUSAL (ruling 2026-10-21, after fleet merge run
    // 39555924538): every receipt is staged flat at the dest root, and ONLY the file the artifact is
    // authoritative for (its must_glob) is copied -- a shard artifact's zip also carries the base the
    // transport staged into the reader's dir before export, and copying the whole tree dropped that
    // copy under dest/shards/, producing the same basename twice (root + nested) so the import batch
    // cp refused ('will not overwrite just-created') and reddened the step. Overwriting a duplicate
    // silently would let a later writer mask an earlier one; instead, a same-named pair whose bytes
    // differ refuses the transport with a typed cause BEFORE the instrument runs. Same bytes = the
    // same fact re-staged, accepted.
    "def place(dest, src):\n",
    "    dst = os.path.join(dest, os.path.basename(src))\n",
    "    if os.path.exists(dst) and open(dst, 'rb').read() != open(src, 'rb').read():\n",
    "        raise SystemExit('wave-transport: transport_duplicate_receipt_bytes_differ: ' + os.path.basename(src) + ' staged twice with different bytes -- refusing before the instrument runs')\n",
    "    shutil.copy2(src, dst)\n",
    "def fetch(wid, dest, want_name, must_glob):\n",
    "    for a in api('/repos/' + REPO + '/actions/runs/' + str(wid) + '/artifacts')['artifacts']:\n",
    "        if a.get('name') != want_name or a.get('expired'):\n",
    "            continue\n",
    "        st = a.get('state')\n",
    "        if st is not None and st != 'completed':\n",
    "            continue\n",
    "        tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=os.path.dirname(dest))\n",
    "        zp = os.path.join(tmp, 'receipt.zip')\n",
    "        download(a['archive_download_url'], zp)\n",
    "        with zipfile.ZipFile(zp) as z:\n",
    "            z.extractall(tmp)\n",
    "        os.remove(zp)\n",
    "        hits = glob.glob(os.path.join(tmp, '**', must_glob), recursive=True)\n",
    "        if not hits:\n",
    "            shutil.rmtree(tmp)\n",
    "            return False\n",
    "        for hit in hits:\n",
    "            place(dest, hit)\n",
    "        shutil.rmtree(tmp)\n",
    "        return True\n",
    "    return False\n",
    "os.makedirs(STAGING, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "clear(STAGING)\n",
    "MB = merge_base()\n",
    "ensure_objects(MB)\n",
    "PROBE = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(STAGING), 'base-probe')\n",
    "if MODE != 'base':\n",
    "    if MODE.startswith('merge/'):\n",
    "        for role in SHARD_ROLES:\n",
    "            for created, wid in artifact_candidates(role, True):\n",
    "                if fetch(wid, STAGING, role_artifact_name(role), 'shard-*-' + role + '.txt'):\n",
    "                    break\n",
    "    for created, wid in artifact_candidates('base', False)[:8]:\n",
    "        clear(PROBE)\n",
    "        if fetch(wid, PROBE, role_artifact_name('base'), 'base-' + MB + '-*' + '.txt'):\n",
    "            for hit in glob.glob(os.path.join(PROBE, '**', 'base-' + MB + '-*' + '.txt'), recursive=True):\n",
    "                place(STAGING, hit)\n",
    "            break\n",
    "    clear(PROBE)\n",
    "PYEOF\n",
  ], "")
}

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_lookup_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Stage the receipts this shape consumes (transport; the reader judges them)" },
    id: Present { value: "wave_receipt_transport" },
    run: instrument_dispatch_wave_lookup_program(),
    shell: Present { value: Bash },
    env: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "GITHUB_TOKEN", value: yaml_string(s: "${{ github.token }}")),
        kv(key: "GUNBC_WAVE_SHARD", value: yaml_string(s: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_expression())),
        kv(key: "WAVE_RECEIPT_STAGING", value: yaml_string(s: join(["${{ runner.temp }}/", instrument_dispatch_directory, "/wave-staging"], ""))),
      ]
    },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: instrument_dispatch_wave_is_wave_expression() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none,
  }
}

// THE READER'S OWN DIRECTORY, MOVED IN -- AND NOTHING ELSE. The receipts the reader reads live under
// the checkout at shard_dir(head_root) (imported, so the directory has one authority: the reader's
// .dag). Before the instrument: the staged receipt files (base-*.txt, shard-*.txt, verdict-*.txt
// ONLY -- an upload carries the run's stdout/stderr too, and strays in the receipt directory are the
// reader's refusal, so they are never moved in). After the instrument: the directory is copied into
// the artifact tree, so receipts survive the runner exiting whatever the exit code was. The copy
// after tolerates an absent directory (a non-wave run never creates it; a refused one may not).
fn instrument_dispatch_shards_in_dir() -> String {
  join(["./", shard_dir(head_root: ".")], "")
}

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_stage_word() -> Node {
  bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [bash_build_word_var(name: "WAVE_RECEIPT_STAGING")])
}

fn instrument_dispatch_shards_dir_word() -> Node {
  bash_build_word_lit(text: instrument_dispatch_shards_in_dir())
}

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_import_statements() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "mkdir"), bash_build_word_lit(text: "-p"), instrument_dispatch_shards_dir_word()]),
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "find"),
      instrument_dispatch_wave_stage_word(),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-type"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "f"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "("),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-name"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "base-*.txt"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-o"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-name"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "shard-*.txt"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-o"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-name"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "verdict-*.txt"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: ")"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-exec"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "cp"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-a"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "-t"),
      instrument_dispatch_shards_dir_word(),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "{}"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "+"),
    ]),
  ]
}

// AFTER the instrument: carry the reader's receipts into the artifact tree so they survive the runner
// exiting -- whatever the exit code was, including the refusals. The whole copy is guarded on the
// directory EXISTING: a non-wave run never creates it, and a run refused before the census writes
// anything may not either; an unguarded `cp -a dir/.` would turn that ordinary absence into a red
// step, and a red of the transport would be read as a red of the instrument.
fn instrument_dispatch_shards_export_statement() -> Node {
  bash_build_if_from_stmts(
    cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "-d"), instrument_dispatch_shards_dir_word()]),
    then_stmts: [
      bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "mkdir"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "-p"),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [instrument_dispatch_dir_word(), bash_build_word_lit(text: "/shards")]),
      ]),
      bash_build_command_with_words(words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "cp"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "-a"),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [instrument_dispatch_shards_dir_word(), bash_build_word_lit(text: "/.")]),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [instrument_dispatch_dir_word(), bash_build_word_lit(text: "/shards")]),
      ]),
    ]
  )
}

fn instrument_dispatch_wave_import_step() -> Step {
  match bash_emit_stmts(statements: instrument_dispatch_wave_import_statements(), set_e: false) {
    Accepted { value: script } => RunStep {
      name: Present { value: "Move the staged receipts into the reader's directory" },
      id: Present { value: "wave_receipt_import" },
      run: script,
      shell: Present { value: Bash },
      env: Present {
        value: [kv(key: "WAVE_RECEIPT_STAGING", value: yaml_string(s: join(["${{ runner.temp }}/", instrument_dispatch_directory, "/wave-staging"], "")))],
      },
      if_condition: Present { value: instrument_dispatch_wave_is_wave_expression() },
      continue_on_error: none,
      timeout_minutes: none,
    }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => instrument_dispatch_wave_import_step()
  }
}

fn instrument_dispatch_shards_export_step() -> Step {
  match bash_emit_stmts(statements: [instrument_dispatch_shards_export_statement()], set_e: false) {
    Accepted { value: script } => RunStep {
      name: Present { value: "Copy the reader's receipts into the artifact tree" },
      id: Present { value: "wave_receipt_export" },
      run: script,
      shell: Present { value: Bash },
      env: none,
      if_condition: Present { value: "always()" },
      continue_on_error: none,
      timeout_minutes: none,
    }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => instrument_dispatch_shards_export_step()
  }
}

fn instrument_dispatch_steps(seed: String, guard: String, script: String) -> List<Step> {
  list_append(
    left: list_append(left: [instrument_dispatch_seed_step(script: seed)], right: list_map(xs: instrument_dispatch_memory_demands(), f: fn(d) { instrument_dispatch_memory_guard_step(d: d, guard: guard) })),
    right: list_append(
      left: list_map(xs: compiler_gate_hosted_build_prelude_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step }),
      right: [
        witness_toolchain_homes_isolated_guard_step(),
        instrument_dispatch_wave_lookup_step(),
        instrument_dispatch_wave_import_step(),
        instrument_dispatch_run_step(script: script),
        instrument_dispatch_shards_export_step(),
        instrument_dispatch_upload_step(),
      ],
    ),
  )
}

fn instrument_dispatch_job(seed: String, guard: String, script: String) -> Job {
  Job {
    id: instrument_dispatch_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    environment: none,
    steps: instrument_dispatch_steps(seed: seed, guard: guard, script: script),
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: none,
    timeout_minutes: Present { value: instrument_dispatch_timeout_minutes },
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: Present {
      value: WorkflowPermissions {
        contents: Present { value: PermRead },
        pull_requests: none,
        issues: none,
        // admitted by the operator ruling 2026-10-19, recorded at the header condition, for the
        // cross-run receipt transport of the namespace-wave gate and nothing else
        actions: Present { value: PermRead },
        id_token: none,
      }
    }
  }
}

fn instrument_dispatch_workflow(seed: String, guard: String, script: String) -> Workflow {
  Workflow {
    name: instrument_dispatch_workflow_name,
    run_name: Present { value: join(["instrument-dispatch ", instrument_dispatch_label_expression()], "") },
    on: [
      WorkflowDispatch {
        inputs: [
          DispatchInput {
            name: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_input,
            // EXTERNAL FACT (GitHub product policy, DESIGN 3): a workflow_dispatch input description
            // longer than 160 characters makes GitHub reject the ENTIRE workflow file as invalid -- the
            // push run dies jobless with the invalid-workflow signature and dispatches refuse 'Workflow
            // does not have workflow_dispatch trigger' (422). Observed on this repo at head fa5fe4d399
            // (2026-10-20): a 255-char description here killed the file; at 106 chars it loads. Keep
            // every dispatch-input description in this file under 160 characters: the next long one
            // refuses at push time, on the fleet, with no diagnostic beyond a jobless run.
            description: Present { value: "Wave gate only: base | i/K | merge/K. none = not a wave run; a wave label with none refuses malformed (2)." },
            required: false,
            default: Present { value: "none" },
            type: InputChoice { options: instrument_dispatch_wave_shard_options() },
          },
          DispatchInput {
            name: instrument_dispatch_label_input,
            description: Present { value: "The rostered instrument to run (gunbc.instrument_targets); the run executes `gunbc test <label>` at the dispatched ref." },
            required: true,
            default: none,
            type: InputChoice { options: instrument_dispatch_label_options() },
          },
        ]
      }
    ],
    concurrency: Present { value: instrument_dispatch_concurrency },
    env: none,
    permissions: Present {
      value: WorkflowPermissions {
        contents: Present { value: PermRead },
        pull_requests: none,
        issues: none,
        actions: none,
        id_token: none,
      }
    },
    jobs: [instrument_dispatch_job(seed: seed, guard: guard, script: script)],
  }
}

type InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationOutcome
  = InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerated { content: String }
  | InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: String }

fn expected_instrument_dispatch_yml() -> InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationOutcome {
  if !instrument_dispatch_memory_demands_are_dispatchable() {
    InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow: a memory demand names a label that is not dispatchable" }
  } else if !instrument_dispatch_memory_demands_fit_selected_target() {
    InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow: a dispatchable label's declared memory demand exceeds the selected runner target's budget (gunbc.ci.ci_runner_target ci_runner_target_ram_speed_budget), so no runner this workflow targets can hold it" }
  } else if instrument_dispatch_labels_are_rostered() {
    match instrument_dispatch_seed_script() {
      Rejected { diagnostics: _ } =>
        InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow: v2.workflow.bash_emit refused the seed script, so no workflow is emitted" }
      Accepted { value: seed, diagnostics: _ } =>
        match instrument_dispatch_script() {
          Rejected { diagnostics: _ } =>
            InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow: v2.workflow.bash_emit refused the run script, so no workflow is emitted" }
          Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } =>
            match instrument_dispatch_memory_guard_script() {
              Rejected { diagnostics: _ } =>
                InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow: v2.workflow.bash_emit refused the memory guard script, so no workflow is emitted" }
              Accepted { value: guard, diagnostics: _ } =>
                expected_instrument_dispatch_yml_with(workflow: instrument_dispatch_workflow(seed: seed, guard: guard, script: script))
            }
        }
    }
  } else {
    InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow: a dispatchable label is not a row of gunbc.instrument_targets instrument_targets" }
  }
}

fn expected_instrument_dispatch_yml_with(workflow: Workflow) -> InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationOutcome {
  match admit_workflow_action_uses(workflow: workflow) {
    WorkflowActionUsesRefused { refusals: rs } =>
      InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: workflow_action_uses_refusal_reason(workflow_module: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow", refusals: rs) }
    WorkflowActionUsesAdmitted =>
      match admit_workflow_checkout_refs(workflow: workflow, declared: []) {
        WorkflowCheckoutRefsRefused { refusals: crs } =>
          InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: workflow_checkout_refs_refusal_reason(workflow_module: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow", refusals: crs) }
        WorkflowCheckoutRefsAdmitted =>
          match admit_workflow_toolchain_homes(workflow: workflow) {
            WorkflowToolchainHomesAdmitted =>
              match emit_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: workflow)) {
                EmittedYaml { text: t } => InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerated { content: t }
                YamlEmitRefused { path: p, reason: r } =>
                  InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: yaml_emit_refusal_text(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow", path: p, reason: r) }
              }
            WorkflowToolchainHomeRefused { job_id: j, refusal: r } =>
              InstrumentDispatchWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: toolchain_home_refusal_reason(job_id: j, refusal: r) }
          }
      }
  }
}
